# What the open-jev image installs, before resolution. The Dockerfile
# installs the installer from requirements-build.lock.txt, then
# requirements.lock.txt, generated from this file, both with
# `pip install --require-hashes --no-deps`, so every package (dependencies
# included) is pinned to an exact version and hash. After changing anything
# here or a revision in the Dockerfile, delete both lock files (uv keeps an
# existing lock's pins) and regenerate them from the kit root:
#
#   uv pip compile runners/open-jev/requirements.in --no-deps --generate-hashes \
#     --python-version 3.12 --python-platform x86_64-manylinux_2_39 \
#     --exclude-newer 2026-10-28 --exclude-newer-package cuda-pathfinder=2026-11-04 \
#     --exclude-newer-package filelock=2026-11-04 --exclude-newer-package hf-xet=2026-11-04 \
#     --output-file runners/open-jev/requirements.lock.txt
#   printf 'pip==26.2.2\n' | uv pip compile - --no-deps --generate-hashes \
#     --python-version 3.12 --python-platform x86_64-manylinux_2_39 --exclude-newer 2026-10-28 \
#     --output-file runners/open-jev/requirements-build.lock.txt
#
# Python 3.12 and glibc 2.39 are the image's Ubuntu 24.04 base. Set
# --exclude-newer to a date at least a week back, so no release younger than
# that enters the lock.
# cuda-pathfinder 1.8.5, filelock 4.2.5 and hf-xet 1.8.0 were published inside
# that week; they are exempted by name because the engine was validated with
# them. With --no-deps every version here is an exact pin, so nothing else can
# enter.

# Exactly the set the engine was validated with, compiled with --no-deps;
# `pip check` in the Dockerfile proves the set is complete. torch 2.13.0 on
# PyPI is the CUDA 13.0 build; its CUDA libraries are the pinned nvidia
# wheels.
